      "message": "Why exactly is it a problem to allow a pVM to disable some of the mitigations if it wants to?\n\nI see Linux pVMs indeed disable SPEC_CTRL_SSBD by default (and so does the host, actually), since Linux kernel sets `ssb_mode \u003d SPEC_STORE_BYPASS_PRCTL` by default, i.e. SPEC_CTRL_SSBD is only enabled when switching to a userspace process that enabled it via prctl(PR_SET_SPECULATION_CTRL).\n\nBut as I understand, there is a reason for that. Frankly I\u0027m very ignorant about how these CPU vulnerabilities work, but I see this in `should_mitigate_vuln()`:\n\n```\n\tcase X86_BUG_SPEC_STORE_BYPASS:\n\t\treturn cpu_attack_vector_mitigated(CPU_MITIGATE_USER_USER);\n```\n\ni.e. SSB only allows attacks from userspace to userspace (within the same guest if this is in a guest), not from guest to guest (in particular, not from the host VM to a pVM)? So forcing enabling it would only cause an unneeded perf penalty?\n\nBTW also it looks like for the same reason we don\u0027t need to enable SPEC_CTRL_SSBD for the hypervisor (in pKVM\u0027s `mitigate_spec_store_bypass()`) either?",
